01. ALB + VPC:
Suppose your application runs on:

EC2 : 8080

You don't want users accessing EC2 directly.

Instead:

Internet
    |
    v
ALB : 443
    |
    v
EC2 : 8080

Security groups:

ALB-SG

Inbound:
443 from 0.0.0.0/0

EC2:

EC2-SG

Inbound:
8080 from ALB-SG

This creates a security boundary:

Internet
   |
   | HTTPS
   v
ALB
   |
   | HTTP : 8080
   v
EC2




02. RDS + VPC:
Your database should generally not be publicly accessible for a typical application architecture.

Example:

Internet
   |
   v
ALB
   |
   v
Application
   |
   v
RDS

Subnets:

Public Subnets
      |
      v
ALB

Private App Subnets
      |
      v
EC2 / EKS

Private DB Subnets
      |
      v
RDS

Security Groups:

ALB-SG
   |
   v
APP-SG
   |
   v
DB-SG

DB-SG:

Inbound:
3306 from APP-SG

Not:

3306 from 0.0.0.0/0



